### 1.2 — Raise your own brood
#### Raise your own brood
- Raise Chaurus and Spider companions. The new Companions tab is optional and off by default.
- Watch them grow from Baby to Juvenile, Adolescent and Adult. The default milestones are 1, 4 and 6 in-game days. Existing preferences are kept.
- Tap Activate on a baby to carry it, or hold Activate on a companion to give it a name. Up to five babies are visible on your back, and each carried baby adds 5 carry weight.
- Babies don't count toward the summon limit. Ownership and active summon limits are configurable.
- With follower adoption enabled, eligible hatchlings from active female followers can join your roster, including when you play a male character.
- Hatchlings can have distinct patterns and colors. There are 480 combinations across both species, with a configurable 35% chance by default. They keep their appearance as they grow.
- Set movement speed separately for each species. Companion spiders gain poison spit at Juvenile.
- Order companions to feed on corpses to shorten their growth time. The body fades as they feed and drops its gear at the end.
#### Commands and summoning
- Use the command wheel to Move, Attack, Defeat, Eat, Wait/Follow, Hop On/Hop Off or Desummon. Preview targets and destinations with animated markers before confirming.
- Choose Passive or Aggressive behavior. Direct orders work in either mode without changing your stance.
- With one key, hold for the wheel, tap then hold to choose how many to summon, or tap once to desummon. You can also bind summoning and desummoning to a separate key.
- Summon with a finger snap and watch companions emerge in staggered bursts of dirt, smoke and shadow. When dismissed, grown companions burrow back underground.
- Optional console commands spawn baby or adult companions and remove only those console spawns, even after they've grown.
#### Companion defeat
- Defeat is a separate, nonlethal order. Adult companions qualify by default; you can also allow Adolescents or Juveniles.
- Defeat looks for a matching SexLab scene with up to four companions of the same species. It supports vaginal and anal-only animations, with an option to include oral-only animations.
- Defeated NPCs drop their weapons and shield. Dropping their main outfit is on by default and can be turned off.
- NPCs stay down for a configurable recovery period after the scene. If no scene can start, they go straight to knockdown recovery.
#### Pregnancy and hatchery
- With Fertility Mode Reloaded installed, choose whether the two systems run independently, Estrus takes priority without interrupting FMR labor, or Estrus skips actors who are already pregnant through FMR. This applies to all three Estrus types.
- Chaurus and Spider eggs are easier to hit and roll fertility only once. Breaking an egg prevents it from hatching.
- Hatchlings grow through the same stages even with Companions off, including NPC-born hatchlings. Wild babies are slower, have growth-scaled health and fight back only if attacked. Older wild offspring become hostile.
#### Performance and fixes
- Faster startup and first-scene setup by removing the full animation-folder scan.
- Separate switches for gameplay, attack and debug notifications. Startup and scene-stage debug messages are off by default.
- Fixed separate-orgasm detection for SLSO and SexLab P+, and fixed Keep Chaurus size during P+ scene setup.
- Fixed some NPC pregnancies stalling without growth, and improved player birth cleanup to prevent movement staying locked after get-up.
- Improved scene recovery when loading a save or when a scene fails. Wild attackers can resume combat after their calm period without the previous crash.
- Corrected recovery timers and Dwemer birth reporting. Fixed older milk effects interfering with newer ones.
#### Compatibility and API
- The FOMOD includes optional patches for Milk Mod Economy pregnancy detection and SexLab Parasites pregnancy and egg detection.
- Mod authors can request pregnancy, delivery with eggs, or hatchable eggs on their own.
- Mods can block new attacks or pregnancies separately, by creature type or for all three, globally or per actor, without changing the player's settings.
- Added actor-specific birth events that distinguish delivery from resets and cancellations. The older Dwemer callback still works.

